## Lakehouse RT (Statement Execution API) support — opt-in per-warehouse toggle

Context: Building the Knowledge Graph against a serverless Lakehouse/RT SQL
warehouse failed at the R2RML VIEW creation step with `BAD_REQUEST: Lakehouse/RT
is not supported for Thrift protocol. Please update your Databricks SQL Driver
version ... which supports the Statement Execution API protocol`. The installed
`databricks-sql-connector` (4.5.0) already supports the Statement Execution API
(SEA) but defaults to the legacy Thrift backend unless the caller passes
`use_sea=True`; RT warehouses reject Thrift. The error was also mis-reported as a
generic "check source tables / permissions" failure, hiding the real cause.

This change adds an opt-in **Lakehouse RT warehouse** checkbox under
`Settings → Lakehouse → SQL Warehouse`. When enabled, the Lakehouse (Delta)
graph client connects via SEA instead of Thrift. The flag is stored next to the
Lakehouse warehouse id in `graph_engine_config.lakehouse.use_sea` and defaults to
`False`, so existing deployments on classic SQL warehouses are unchanged.

Changes:
1. src/back/core/graphdb/engine_config.py
   Added a `_coerce_bool` helper; `_finalize_lakehouse_bucket` now stamps a
   normalized `use_sea` boolean onto a non-empty Lakehouse bucket (empty buckets
   stay `{}` to preserve existing contracts). Added public
   `resolve_lakehouse_use_sea(cfg)` (default `False`).
2. src/back/objects/session/GlobalConfigService.py
   Added `get_delta_warehouse_use_sea(...)`; extended `set_delta_warehouse_id(...)`
   with an optional `use_sea` parameter that is written alongside `warehouse_id`
   (and preserved when omitted).
3. src/back/core/helpers/DatabricksHelpers.py, src/back/core/helpers/__init__.py
   Added `resolve_lakehouse_use_sea(domain, settings)` (default `False`, bypasses
   the falsy-swallowing `_resolve_global_setting`, mirroring
   `resolve_use_cloud_fetch`) and re-exported it at package level.
4. src/back/core/databricks/DatabricksAuth.py
   `__init__` accepts `use_sea`; `get_sql_connection_params()` adds
   `use_sea=True` only when enabled; the CloudFetch probe params mirror the flag.
5. src/back/core/databricks/DatabricksClient.py
   `__init__` accepts and forwards `use_sea` to `DatabricksAuth`.
6. src/back/core/graphdb/delta/DeltaBase.py
   `create_databricks_client` resolves `resolve_lakehouse_use_sea(...)` and passes
   `use_sea` to the Delta `DatabricksClient` (session-less branch reads
   `databricks.use_sea`).
7. src/back/objects/domain/SettingsService.py
   `select_delta_warehouse(..., *, use_sea=False)` persists and returns `use_sea`;
   `get_delta_warehouse_result(...)` returns the saved `use_sea`.
8. src/api/routers/internal/settings.py
   `POST /settings/select-delta-warehouse` reads `use_sea` from the request body.
9. src/front/templates/settings.html, src/front/static/config/js/settings.js
   Added the "Lakehouse RT warehouse" checkbox on the SQL Warehouse tab; it loads
   from `GET /settings/delta-warehouse` and is saved with the existing **Apply**
   button (`use_sea` in the POST body).
10. src/back/core/databricks/SQLWarehouse.py
    `_create_or_replace` (VIEW/TABLE DDL) now maps the RT "not supported for
    Thrift protocol" `BAD_REQUEST` to an actionable message pointing at the new
    checkbox, instead of the generic failure text.
11. pyproject.toml
    Bumped version 0.8.0 → 0.8.1.

